WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.571
Welcome. This class is about choosing
orchestration because the work

2
00:00:05.571 --> 00:00:11.143
requires a particular control structure,
not because a larger collection

3
00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:16.714
of agents sounds more capable. We will use
a dependency-free

4
00:00:16.714 --> 00:00:22.286
local Node.js lab containing seven
executable patterns: direct execution, a

5
00:00:22.286 --> 00:00:27.857
fixed sequence, conditional router
dispatch, a manager that retains control,

6
00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:33.429
a trusted handoff and return, asynchronous
parallel branches with a

7
00:00:33.429 --> 00:00:39.000
required join, and a bounded
evaluator-reviser loop. The fixtures are

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.571
deterministic local functions. They make
no provider request and execute

9
00:00:44.571 --> 00:00:50.143
no hosted model. That boundary matters
because the lab demonstrates

10
00:00:50.143 --> 00:00:55.714
orchestration behavior, contracts,
concurrency, and failure handling, but

11
00:00:55.714 --> 00:01:01.286
does not demonstrate provider quality. By
the end, you should

12
00:01:01.286 --> 00:01:06.857
be able to select the smallest sufficient
pattern, identify who

13
00:01:06.857 --> 00:01:12.429
controls each transition, prove whether
work really overlaps, and explain

14
00:01:12.429 --> 00:01:18.000
why a terminal result is permitted.

15
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.059
Begin with direct execution. In the direct
lab graph, one

16
00:01:23.059 --> 00:01:28.118
parent fixture performs a bounded publish
action and its validated

17
00:01:28.118 --> 00:01:33.176
result determines the terminal state. This
shape minimizes latency, state

18
00:01:33.176 --> 00:01:38.235
transfer, permissions, and recovery paths.
Move to a sequential workflow

19
00:01:38.235 --> 00:01:43.294
only when there is a real dependency. The
sequential demo

20
00:01:43.294 --> 00:01:48.353
creates a draft, validates that draft, and
then lets the

21
00:01:48.353 --> 00:01:53.412
parent aggregate current validation
evidence. Validation cannot correctly

22
00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:58.471
start before the draft exists, so the edge
is justified.

23
00:01:58.471 --> 00:02:03.529
For every additional boundary, ask what it
buys. A specialist

24
00:02:03.529 --> 00:02:08.588
might narrow permissions, reduce
conflicting instructions, or make

25
00:02:08.588 --> 00:02:13.647
evaluation easier. It also creates another
contract, another budget reservation,

26
00:02:13.647 --> 00:02:18.706
and another failure surface. If a step is
fully known

27
00:02:18.706 --> 00:02:23.765
and deterministic, keep it in application
code. If one bounded

28
00:02:23.765 --> 00:02:28.824
operation solves the task, do not build a
manager, handoff

29
00:02:28.824 --> 00:02:33.882
network, or review loop around it.
Simplicity is not the

30
00:02:33.882 --> 00:02:38.941
absence of governance. Direct execution
still needs authority checks, contracts,

31
00:02:38.941 --> 00:02:44.000
timeouts, budgets, result validation, and
an explicit terminal state.

32
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.353
Now consider conditional routing. The
local classifier receives a proposal

33
00:02:49.353 --> 00:02:54.706
with a risk field. It may return only
specialist or

34
00:02:54.706 --> 00:03:00.059
direct because those are the two keys in a

35
00:03:00.059 --> 00:03:05.412
trusted route map. With the supplied
high-risk input, the classifier

36
00:03:05.412 --> 00:03:10.765
selects specialist. The executor records
the selection, marks directWorker as

37
00:03:10.765 --> 00:03:16.118
skipped, and dispatches specialistWorker
only. The final join requires successful

38
00:03:16.118 --> 00:03:21.471
current-revision evidence from that
selected worker. Change the input risk

39
00:03:21.471 --> 00:03:26.824
to low and the invoked worker changes to
directWorker while

40
00:03:26.824 --> 00:03:32.176
specialistWorker is skipped. This is real
dispatch, not a label

41
00:03:32.176 --> 00:03:37.529
attached after both workers run. If the
classifier returns admin,

42
00:03:37.529 --> 00:03:42.882
an empty value, or another unknown value,
execution fails with

43
00:03:42.882 --> 00:03:48.235
ROUTE_INVALID before either branch or
approval. The classifier is therefore

44
00:03:48.235 --> 00:03:53.588
allowed to select among declared
capabilities, not invent a capability.

45
00:03:53.588 --> 00:03:58.941
Use this pattern when an input-dependent
semantic decision provides enough

46
00:03:58.941 --> 00:04:04.294
benefit to justify classifier error, route
observability, and closed-map validation.

47
00:04:04.294 --> 00:04:09.647
Use ordinary conditional code when a
reliable deterministic rule already

48
00:04:09.647 --> 00:04:15.000
expresses the choice.

49
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.176
The first three patterns establish a
progression. Direct has one

50
00:04:20.176 --> 00:04:25.353
bounded operation. Sequential encodes true
ordered dependencies. Router dispatches

51
00:04:25.353 --> 00:04:30.529
exactly one branch selected from trusted
state. The graph, rather

52
00:04:30.529 --> 00:04:35.706
than a worker, owns the allowed edges.
This principle also

53
00:04:35.706 --> 00:04:40.882
governs loops. A loop is not permission to
continue until

54
00:04:40.882 --> 00:04:46.059
a worker feels satisfied. It needs
measurable criteria, a revision

55
00:04:46.059 --> 00:04:51.235
counter, budget and deadline checks, and
declared terminal behavior. Google

56
00:04:51.235 --> 00:04:56.412
ADK documents sequential, loop, and
parallel workflow agents as deterministic

57
00:04:56.412 --> 00:05:01.588
workflow structures. The portable lesson
is not that every framework

58
00:05:01.588 --> 00:05:06.765
uses the same class names. It is that
known control

59
00:05:06.765 --> 00:05:11.941
flow belongs in code. A model or other
uncertain worker

60
00:05:11.941 --> 00:05:17.118
may produce a classification, draft,
evaluation, or revision, but it

61
00:05:17.118 --> 00:05:22.294
should not invent hidden edges, silently
add retries, or create

62
00:05:22.294 --> 00:05:27.471
a new success state. Record the input and
output contract

63
00:05:27.471 --> 00:05:32.647
on each edge so a trace can show which

64
00:05:32.647 --> 00:05:37.824
interface governed the transition. Record
the route reason, budget reservation,

65
00:05:37.824 --> 00:05:43.000
accepted result revision, and terminal
decision for the same reason.

66
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.947
Manager and handoff patterns differ
primarily in who remains active.

67
00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:52.895
In the manager demo, the parent calls a
research specialist

68
00:05:52.895 --> 00:05:57.842
as a tool. The specialist may read and
return evidence,

69
00:05:57.842 --> 00:06:02.789
but it cannot approve. The parent retains
control, validates the

70
00:06:02.789 --> 00:06:07.737
artifact, synthesizes the result, and owns
approval. This arrangement is

71
00:06:07.737 --> 00:06:12.684
useful when a central agent must preserve
one user-facing plan

72
00:06:12.684 --> 00:06:17.632
and combine several specialist artifacts.
Its risks include a manager

73
00:06:17.632 --> 00:06:22.579
bottleneck and overconfidence in evidence
it cannot judge. In the

74
00:06:22.579 --> 00:06:27.526
handoff demo, ownership actually changes.
The parent transfers to

75
00:06:27.526 --> 00:06:32.474
security-specialist under a declared
transfer contract. The specialist becomes

76
00:06:32.474 --> 00:06:37.421
active, performs its bounded
responsibility, and returns ownership

77
00:06:37.421 --> 00:06:42.368
under a separate return contract. Approval
is possible only after

78
00:06:42.368 --> 00:06:47.316
ownership returns to parent. A fixture
result that claims transfer

79
00:06:47.316 --> 00:06:52.263
is not sufficient. The executor checks the
trusted source, destination,

80
00:06:52.263 --> 00:06:57.211
transition, principal, and acceptance
contract. Use manager delegation when the

81
00:06:57.211 --> 00:07:02.158
parent must retain synthesis and
conversational control. Use handoff when

82
00:07:02.158 --> 00:07:07.105
the specialist needs to own the next phase
under narrower

83
00:07:07.105 --> 00:07:12.053
instructions. Do not use these names
interchangeably because their state,

84
00:07:12.053 --> 00:07:17.000
permission, and recovery requirements
differ.

85
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.250
Checkpoint. A support coordinator asks a
security specialist for an

86
00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:25.500
analysis, receives the typed report, and
remains responsible for the

87
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.750
final customer response. Is this a manager
or a handoff?

88
00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:34.000
Choose manager because active control
never left the coordinator. Now

89
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:38.250
change one fact: the specialist becomes
the active owner and

90
00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:42.500
communicates through a narrower security
workflow until an explicit return

91
00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:46.750
condition. That is a handoff. Your answer
should depend on

92
00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:51.000
control ownership, not on whether two
workers exist.

93
00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:56.222
Parallelism is useful only when branches
are independent. Independence means

94
00:07:56.222 --> 00:08:01.444
more than different names or different
models. One branch must

95
00:08:01.444 --> 00:08:06.667
not require another branch's result, both
must not mutate the

96
00:08:06.667 --> 00:08:11.889
same state without coordination, and they
must not consume an

97
00:08:11.889 --> 00:08:17.111
unsafe shared scarce limit. In the lab,
research, review, and

98
00:08:17.111 --> 00:08:22.333
validation receive the same input revision
and have empty dependency

99
00:08:22.333 --> 00:08:27.556
lists. The scheduler starts their promises
without awaiting one before

100
00:08:27.556 --> 00:08:32.778
starting the next. Tests record active
fixture counts and start

101
00:08:32.778 --> 00:08:38.000
events, proving that the three operations
overlap and may finish

102
00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:43.222
out of order. The parent does not accept
whichever result

103
00:08:43.222 --> 00:08:48.444
finishes last. It joins evidence by
dependency ID and requires

104
00:08:48.444 --> 00:08:53.667
research, review, and validation to
succeed at the current revision.

105
00:08:53.667 --> 00:08:58.889
If one required branch fails, approval is
prevented. A sibling

106
00:08:58.889 --> 00:09:04.111
completing after the terminal failure is
marked as an ignored

107
00:09:04.111 --> 00:09:09.333
late completion. This distinction is
central: concurrency is how work

108
00:09:09.333 --> 00:09:14.556
executes, while the join defines the
product outcome. Decide required

109
00:09:14.556 --> 00:09:19.778
and optional branches, timeout,
cancellation, retry ownership,

110
00:09:19.778 --> 00:09:25.000
disagreement handling, and partial-result
labeling before launching work.

111
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:29.429
Consider two branches that both edit the
same policy document.

112
00:09:29.429 --> 00:09:33.857
Different models perform the edits, and
each branch is individually

113
00:09:33.857 --> 00:09:38.286
fast. Should they run in parallel by
default? Write down

114
00:09:38.286 --> 00:09:42.714
your answer and the state property that
supports it. Then

115
00:09:42.714 --> 00:09:47.143
identify one safe redesign: serialize the
writes, partition the document

116
00:09:47.143 --> 00:09:51.571
into nonoverlapping owned regions, or have
both branches return read-only

117
00:09:51.571 --> 00:09:56.000
proposals for a deterministic
reconciliation step.

118
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.333
The safe answer is no. Different workers
do not make

119
00:11:30.333 --> 00:11:34.667
shared mutation independent. If both
branches can write the same

120
00:11:34.667 --> 00:11:39.000
object, settlement order could silently
determine the artifact or create

121
00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:43.333
an unresolved conflict. The lab rejects
conflicting writes rather than

122
00:11:43.333 --> 00:11:47.667
treating completion order as authority.
Serialization is appropriate when the

123
00:11:47.667 --> 00:11:52.000
second edit depends on the first.
Partitioning is appropriate only

124
00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:56.333
when ownership boundaries truly do not
overlap. Proposal-only branches can

125
00:11:56.333 --> 00:12:00.667
overlap because a separate join owns
reconciliation. Also avoid the

126
00:12:00.667 --> 00:12:05.000
opposite mistake of rejecting all
concurrency. Independent read-only

127
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:09.333
evidence collection can overlap safely
when each branch has isolated

128
00:12:09.333 --> 00:12:13.667
scope and the join explicitly handles
required results, disagreement, timeout,

129
00:12:13.667 --> 00:12:18.000
cancellation, and partial failure.

130
00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:23.647
The seventh pattern is the
evaluator-reviser loop. The supplied

131
00:12:23.647 --> 00:12:29.294
artifact begins without two required
criteria: signed release records and

132
00:12:29.294 --> 00:12:34.941
a verified rollback owner. Evaluation zero
returns approved false, the

133
00:12:34.941 --> 00:12:40.588
exact unmet criteria, feedback, and
artifact revision zero. The reviser

134
00:12:40.588 --> 00:12:46.235
uses that feedback to add one missing
criterion and produces

135
00:12:46.235 --> 00:12:51.882
artifact revision one. Evaluation one
still finds the second criterion

136
00:12:51.882 --> 00:12:57.529
missing, so a second revision changes the
artifact. Evaluation two

137
00:12:57.529 --> 00:13:03.176
sees both criteria and explicitly
approves. The demo therefore contains

138
00:13:03.176 --> 00:13:08.824
three evaluations and two revisions.
Approval cannot come from the

139
00:13:08.824 --> 00:13:14.471
reviser. An evaluator claim with approved
true is rejected if

140
00:13:14.471 --> 00:13:20.118
its unmet list is not empty. If the
maximum revision

141
00:13:20.118 --> 00:13:25.765
count is reached before approval, the
terminal state is escalation,

142
00:13:25.765 --> 00:13:31.412
not success. Budget exhaustion, operation
timeout, or loop deadline also

143
00:13:31.412 --> 00:13:37.059
escalates. Use this pattern when quality
can be expressed as

144
00:13:37.059 --> 00:13:42.706
stable criteria and revisions can make
observable progress. Do not

145
00:13:42.706 --> 00:13:48.353
use it for subjective endless polishing.
Criteria, revision cap, budget,

146
00:13:48.353 --> 00:13:54.000
deadline, and escalation owner must be
declared before execution.

147
00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:59.647
Across all seven patterns, observability
and limits make the control

148
00:13:59.647 --> 00:14:05.294
structure enforceable. Record the run
identifier, active owner, node and

149
00:14:05.294 --> 00:14:10.941
parent span, route decision, contract
versions, input revision, accepted

150
00:14:10.941 --> 00:14:16.588
evidence, effects, reservations, retries,
and terminal state. Keep secrets and

151
00:14:16.588 --> 00:14:22.235
unnecessary personal data out of
telemetry. A trace should still

152
00:14:22.235 --> 00:14:27.882
let an authorized reviewer reconstruct why
execution moved and why

153
00:14:27.882 --> 00:14:33.529
it ended. Every lab node declares a
tenant, role, principal,

154
00:14:33.529 --> 00:14:39.176
allowed actions, reservation, timeout,
attempt cap, input contract, and output

155
00:14:39.176 --> 00:14:44.824
contract. Validation rejects unknown
dependencies, duplicate IDs, cycles,

156
00:14:44.824 --> 00:14:50.471
multiple final nodes, stale completions,
dependency contract mismatches, forged

157
00:14:50.471 --> 00:14:56.118
approval, spoofed handoffs, and a final
node that does not

158
00:14:56.118 --> 00:15:01.765
transitively depend on all required work.
These checks are not

159
00:15:01.765 --> 00:15:07.412
decorative. They prevent a fast or hostile
result from bypassing

160
00:15:07.412 --> 00:15:13.059
the graph. Frameworks expose different
manager, handoff, workflow, and tool

161
00:15:13.059 --> 00:15:18.706
abstractions. Treat those as adapters.
Preserve the same product-level invariants

162
00:15:18.706 --> 00:15:24.353
and tests when changing implementations
rather than assuming similarly named

163
00:15:24.353 --> 00:15:30.000
SDK objects provide identical guarantees.

164
00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.885
Let us run the local lab. First enter cd

165
00:15:33.885 --> 00:15:37.769
